I would not recommend the eucalyptus leaf, except if you have no other 
means.  It may knock you unconscious as any food, and then you are dead.

If you have a blessed unicorn horn, you should always use it in the 
first attempt to heal yourself and cast cure sickness only in the 
second, i.e. only if the unicorn horn does not work.  (Can one cast cure 
sickness successfully while stunned?)  If the unicorn horn does not 
work, don't waste a swing at Demogorgon, heal yourself immediately. 
(Maybe, you could still safely do so, but I am not sure.)

As soon as you are not stunned, write Elbereth.  Then, if you have a 
(blessed) scroll of earth, read it.  Demogorgon will wander off after 
being hit by a boulder, if he cannot attack you anyway.  You should now 
have bought precious time.  Alternatively, once you have engraved 
Elbereth, levitate, pound on Demo till he goes to the stairs, 
unlevitate, and dig yourself through to the next level.  This also gives 
you time.

Once you are in good shape again, read a cursed scroll of teleport to 
get to the level above him.  Go down the stairs unburdened, with either 
a silver saber or an artifact effective against him or a cockatrice 
corpse.  I don't know whether he will always displace you from the 
stairs or whether there is a chance that you have the stairs for 
yourself.  If you hit him with a cockatrice corpse, it does not make any 
difference.  If you want to hit him with s.th. else, you should make 
sure you are on the stairs and either drop a scroll of scare monster (if 
you had that you probably would have done so already :-), this is 
independent of being stunned!) or write Elbereth again.   If you are not 
on the stairs, you have to decide whether to chase him upstairs 
(probably the easiest way, as you separate him from Yeenoghu and, if the 
level above is a teleport level, you can teleport to the stairs 
immediately after moving upstairs yourself) or to try to gain the stairs 
while you are fighting him.  Again you should use Elbereth generously, 
if you can.

